  "knowledgePattern": "Combine the leading-coefficient and strict discriminant conditions",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Check which way the graph opens",
      "body": "<p>Sketch an upward-opening parabola and a downward-opening one. Which could stay above the x-axis for every x?</p>",
      "recap": "The leading term controls the ends of a polynomial graph.",
      "pitfall": "A zero leading coefficient makes this a linear expression.",
      "pause": "Use the coefficient of x² to restrict k, then check k=0 separately.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-polynomial-root-signs",
          "reminder": "The sign of a quadratic’s x² coefficient decides which way it opens."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Keep the curve above the axis",
      "body": "<p>For the remaining quadratic, calculate its discriminant. Decide whether touching the horizontal axis is allowed by the strict inequality.</p>",
      "recap": "The discriminant tells you how many real roots a quadratic has.",
      "pitfall": "Positive means strictly greater than zero.",
      "pause": "Factor the discriminant and solve the conditions together.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l03",
          "reminder": "For a quadratic, a zero discriminant means the curve touches the x-axis."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the quadratic is negative for sufficiently large positive or negative x. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the expression becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, which is not positive for every x. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is necessary.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> For an upward-opening quadratic, strict positivity everywhere requires no real roots: its discriminant must be negative. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> is positive, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> precisely when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Conversely these two conditions make the whole quadratic strictly positive. Answer A. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, which vanishes and fails the strict inequality.</p>"
